<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Ruanda-Urundi</h1><p class="st-meta">1916 CE – 1962 CE · capital Usumbura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Belgian mandate over the kingdoms of Rwanda and Burundi, whose colonial policies sharpened the Hutu and Tutsi divide.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Belgian troops from the Congo occupied the north-western part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a> in 1916. After the war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> assigned the territory to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Belgium</a> as a mandate, confirmed in 1924, and in 1946 it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory. Its administrative centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Usumbura</a>, now Bujumbura.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Belgium</a> ruled indirectly through the mwami of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-rwanda">Kingdom of Rwanda</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-burundi">Kingdom of Burundi</a> and their chiefs, favouring a Tutsi elite and replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> chiefs. Identity cards introduced in the 1930s fixed ethnic labels. The Catholic Church ran schools and missions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> became the main cash crop, often grown under compulsion. The territory was administered jointly with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/belgian-congo">Belgian Congo</a> from 1925.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1959 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> revolution in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-rwanda">Rwanda</a> overthrew the monarchy, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-burundi">Burundi</a> stayed a kingdom. The UN failed to keep the two together, and on 1 July 1962 they became independent as the republic of Rwanda and the Kingdom of Burundi.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ruanda-urundi.jpg" alt="Ruanda-Urundi" width="320" height="277"><figcaption>Image: Dbenbenn and others · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belgium.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1916 CE – 1962 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Usumbura</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kinyarwanda, Kirundi, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/rwanda">Rwanda</a>, <a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Belgian Ruanda-Urundi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1939&amp;focus=ruanda-urundi" aria-label="Ruanda-Urundi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5510 2279 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ruanda-Urundi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5830 2535L5826 2522M5830 2535L5827 2571M5830 2535L5099 670M5830 2535L6087 2688M5830 2535L5836 2507"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5826 2522h0M5827 2571h0M5099 670h0M6087 2688h0M5836 2507h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5830 2535h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5830 2535h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1939&amp;focus=ruanda-urundi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
